# Source Policy

Use this rule whenever technical research, debugging, implementation, design, or explanation depends
on external information sources.

## Qiita and Zenn are forbidden

Do not use Qiita or Zenn as information sources.

Forbidden domains:

- `qiita.com`
- `*.qiita.com`
- `zenn.dev`
- `*.zenn.dev`

Rules:

- Do not open Qiita or Zenn pages.
- Do not summarize Qiita or Zenn articles.
- Do not cite Qiita or Zenn.
- Do not use Qiita or Zenn content as evidence or as the basis for an implementation direction.
- If search results include Qiita or Zenn, ignore those results and look for another source.
- When searching the web, add exclusions whenever practical:
  - `-site:qiita.com`
  - `-site:zenn.dev`

## Preferred sources

Prefer sources in this order:

1. Official documentation
2. Official GitHub repositories
3. RFCs, specifications, and standards
4. Official issues or discussions
5. Maintainer blogs or release notes
6. Trusted company engineering blogs
7. Stack Overflow only as a supplementary source for practical troubleshooting

If no sufficient evidence can be found without Qiita or Zenn, state that no primary or trustworthy
source was found. Do not use Qiita or Zenn as a fallback.

## Security standards

Use the [official OWASP ASVS project](https://owasp.org/projects/asvs) as an authoritative
security-standard reference. At audit execution time, verify the latest stable release against the
[official releases](https://github.com/OWASP/ASVS/releases); do not treat Bleeding Edge as stable.
Record the exact release version, tag, and source commit used by the audit. Qualify requirement
identifiers with that version (for example, `v5.0.0-6.3.3`); chapter numbers are not release versions.
